I'm delighted to be here as time has  gone on  so frequently the oldest person in the  room there was in fact yesterday a rumor  going around that I had served at the  Last Supper I want you to know that's  not true  actually my career began in service to  Ramses the second favor or evening as as  you may know I had served as the  founding president of the National  Commission on certification of  physicians assistants I will really one  anecdote there's an error in at least  one of the textbooks that talks about  the history the Commission was actually  founded on August 8 1974 which is  enshrined in history books not because  of the Commission but because that was  the day that Richard Nixon resigned the  presidency and as we assembled that  morning knowing that he was going to  address the nation that night we were in  a hurry but we did have one piece of  business to transact and that was what  were we going to call these people who  were being certified as physicians  assistants CPA didn't seem reasonable  and so we quickly decided that we would  do PA - see five months later we had  identified Dave Glazer to be the first  executive director of the Commission and  he had opened his office in Atlanta and  on the first or second day that he was  there he received telephone call from  the executive director of the Certified  Public Accountants in this country very  curious after a little chitchat about  what are you going to call these people  and Dave said well we've already made  that determination they're gonna be  called PA - see and he said thank God  now all I have to worry about of the  cotton producers of America  the establishment and the growth and  development of the physician assistant  concept evolved from a number of factors  but principally from the perceived  shortage of health manpower specifically  physicians through the 1950s and 1960s  now whether there was a true shortage of  physicians is arguable what's not  arguable that positions available to  provide general care to the public was  in steep decline in 1943 of for  physicians identified themselves as  generalists twenty years later it was  only one in three while the ratio the  number of physicians of the general  population hadn't changed substantially  their distribution and practice profiles  had changed dramatically one factor was  the urbanization of physicians and the  population after World War Two which  deprived suburban and rural communities  of health manpower a second factor was  the explosive growth of medical  knowledge after World War two  particularly with the establishment of  the NIH in 1948 which led to an  exponential growth of information and  Technology physicians were increasingly  uncomfortable with the breadth of  knowledge that was necessary to practice  general medicine now while there had  been a few elite institutions that was  were devoted to the training of  specialists a major impetus was given  with Eugene Stead's arrival at Duke in  the mid  1940's dead is responsible for more  things than simply the creation of the  concept of the physician assistant when  he arrived from Emory he brought with  him a collection of men because most of  the faculty in those days were men who  later wound up populating many of the  academic institutions in this country  and instead suggested to them that they  might develop a departmental project and  what he had in mind was his blood logo  and as they thought about it they  divided it up Jim Warren later chairman  of Medicine at Ohio State took the heart  itself  John Hickam later chairman of medicine  at the University of Indiana took the  lung Jack Myers my mentor later chairman  at the University of Pittsburgh took the  GI tract and particularly the liver they  received grant funding from the National  Institutes of Health and began to  recruit residents out of the Duke au  staff to fellowships and the concept of  fellowships grew very rapidly with the  dissemination of much of the Duke  faculty throughout the rest of the  country and within a matter of a very  few years specialty fellowship became  the norm for training in internal  medicine by the mid sixties two  Commission's were sanctioned by the  American Medical Association to address  this problem and the consequences for  the public one of them was by John  Millis hmm who was president of Western  Reserve University charged with studying  Graduate Medical Education  that Commission evolved the concept and  the definition of primary care defined  it to include psychiatry office  orthopaedics office gynecology as  augmentations to the traditional  internal medicine or pediatric  residences the second was by dr. Willard  to study the educational preparation for  general practice they recommended a new  specialty of Family Medicine to replace  general practice with the Graduate  training curriculum and a new board of  family physicians to certify training in  this new specialty that was that report  in 1969 was very quickly responded to  with the creation of the American  Association of family practice out of  the American Association of general  practice and the creation of a new board  of family physicians 1966 the felony of  specialization was compounded by  Medicare and Medicaid that established  different reimbursement criteria for  procedures and time related services  such as the history and physical and  cemented forever the the trend toward  specialization in medicine the first  recorded public advocacy of a new kind  of health provider to fill the gap that  had occurred in physician manpower came  in 1961 when Charles Hudson who was then  president of the National Board of  Medical Examiner's spoke to the AMA  House of Delegates advocating providing  assistance to physicians whom he turned  externs now within the American Medical  Association that was surprisingly  well-received now Eugene stead knew all  about this in fact when he had been at  Emory prior to moving up to Duke  University  he was chair of Medicine during World  War two at a time when the number of  interns at the Grady Hospital declined  from 15 to zero and he then appointed  senior medical students to act instead  of interns and termed them externs the  same term that Hudson had used before  the AMA at the same time in North  Carolina there was a remarkable  physician and close friend of stead by  the name of Amos Johnson he was  exceedingly well known he served as one  of the first presidents of the American  Academy of Family Physicians and served  as president of the American Board of  Family Practice during its formative  years he had trained a guy by the name  of Buddy Treadwell to serve him as an  assistant and proclaimed to the rest of  the world that the only reason he could  participate in national activities to  the extent that he did was that Buddy  Treadwell was back there practicing  medicine handling his private practice  in his absence in the early sixties  instead having had many conversations  with Amos Johnson and working with a  nurse by the name of Selma Engels  developed a program at Duke University  to Train nurses to fill this role now he  should  known better because Hudson in his  address to the AMA had had suggested  that that was a possible solution but  expressed sincere skepticism that the  American Nurses Association would ever  go along with this and in the absence of  the AAA going along with it there was no  way it would ultimately work  particularly in the hospital setting and  had suggested that one might use trained  military corpsman who were increasingly  being produced as we were escalating our  small little altercation in Vietnam  predictably I think the program  developed developed by instead and  angles to trained nurses for this came  to an end when the National League for  nurses absolutely refused to accredit it  because these nurses had not been  trained by other nurses but had been  trained by physicians either in practice  or in residency and so instead took the  other option and turned to the folk who  were now coming out of the military and  they were increasing in number by the  year as our escalation in Vietnam  accelerated ultimately amounting to  seven thousand a year coming out of  Vietnam into the civilian population  with without any analogous occupation  and the civilian economy to employ them  a faculty committee was appointed at  Duke University under the chairmanship  of Andrew Wallace it was from Andy  Wallace that I learned about this  in 1966 the committee had deliberated  several members of the committee were in  fact ex-military and had worked with  Corman approval was given in the program  was begun in 1965 early in 1966 picking  up on this before the first class even  graduated Look magazine published an  article called more than a nurse and  less than a doctor which enraged all of  Nursing but attracted several hundred  applications for the second class to  enter Duke University as Andy was  telling me about this I had not thought  of it prior to 1966 there are a few  things in my life that had been so  instantly intuitive as the idea of a  trained assistant of the primary care  physician within those very first couple  of years there were competing models  certainly one of them was in Seattle  where dick Smith created the medics  concept again drawing on ex-military  corpsman more seasoned corpsman who had  been in in independent Duty corpsman for  a number of years at Alderson Broadus  concept arose of taking people out of  high school putting them into a college  curriculum giving them arts and sciences  as well as the medical training and they  emerged after four years leading to a  baccalaureate degree  John Kirkland at Alabama created the  surgical assistant and in Colorado we  had the pediatric assistant now  ultimately these had to be rationalized  but each managed to learn from the other  and ideas from each of these five models  enhanced the concept astoundingly the  concept was formally endorsed by the  American Medical Association House of  Delegates in 1969 I say astoundingly  American Medical Association may have  had another motivation for doing this  and that is the threat of what was being  projected as increased numbers of  medical graduates throughout the country  who might then compete with them in  practice nevertheless they did accept  the concept with an astonishing  resolution that encouraged legislators  and state medical boards to amend their  practice acts to sanction PA practice  the politics of this were were  extraordinary Estes repeatedly stated  that diagnosis and treatment were not  the role of the physician's assistant  that history-taking physical examination  and tasks ordered by the physician were  the goal of objective of physician  assistant training but he knew better  Duke was training him to do more than  that  but he never said so publicly and  instead was unbending on his insistence  on a dependent status and the political  statements coming out of Duke of course  were very effective in allowing the AMA  to make this public declaration in  support of the whole concept  worrisome to the AMA was the fact that  Carnegie had also already proposed the  expansion of medical education to  produce 50% more physicians by 1980 that  had to be terrified  the AMA action however was followed  within the year by formal endorsement by  the American College of Surgeons the  American Academy of Pediatrics and the  American Academy of Family Practice  which brings us to 1971  which was a highly significant year not  least of which because Yale started its  first physician assistant program under  the leadership of Fred and Blair Sadler  who were joined here by and bliss now  Fred and Blair as they will tell you a  little later  I had begun studying the idea of  physicians extenders when they were in  the executive offices of the National  Institute of Health the preceding year  had developed a preliminary paper in  1971 that emerged as a foundation  supported white paper that became the  book the physician assistant today and  tomorrow but in addition to the Yale PA  program and Fred and Blair and Anne's  book there were other things happening  in 1971 first of all in a quite  remarkable bipartisan effort the  Congress passed the comprehensive health  manpower training Act which did a number  of things it it intended to Moncrieff  physician supply by supplying money to  schools to create increased class size  money to develop new schools of medicine  in this country and money to reduce the  length of training  the length of medical education  decidedly ill-advised they were trying  to promote medical schools to go from  four years to three years by eliminating  summers and many medical schools did  that I don't know why anyone thought  that was a good idea you get a square  wave of one year of graduates and you're  done and then you're now compelled to  run all medical schools year round those  medical schools that did adopt that  concept reversed it was in the next few  years there now none of them attempt  anymore to graduate people in three  years but more importantly they provided  money to stimulate new types of  providers and explicitly physician's  assistants now there had been a few  schools largely Foundation and or  federally supported through one granting  agency or another that emulated the five  prototype programs at Duke Seattle  Colorado and Bureau of Health manpower  in 1972 provided money for 36 new PA  programs instantly quadrupling the  number of programs in one year meanwhile  the AMA took new actions their council  on medical education under the direction  of Bill ruie through its Advisory  Committee for allied health  professionals had developed a document  called essentials for an accredited  program for the assistant to the primary  care physician which was roundly  endorsed by the AMA House of Delegates  establishing the joint review committee  that ultimately morphed as we know it  today as arc PA at the same time they  authorized their council of health  manpower to major councils at the AMA  under the direction of Malcolm Todd to  work with others in the development of a  program of certification of physician  assistant graduates of now to be 45:46  programs that were in the pipeline at  the same time the national board of  medical examiners  appointed a committee on goals and  priorities to make recommendations to  their board for the future there's  nothing like being in the right place at  the right time I was asked to be a  member of that goals and priorities  committee and within six months we made  the recommendation to the board that  they undertake for the very first time  in their history the task of examining  professionals other than physicians  specifically the physician's assistant  that was adopted by the board in March  of 1972 and within 18 months the first  certifying examination was born what we  now have in the physician assistant  profession are the four key elements  legs of a chair on which of may a  profession sits if you will first of all  one of those legs is a professional  association the american academy of  physicians assistants have been created  at duke by the graduates of the first  two programs in 1968 with its first  president bill Stanhope in 1972 Duke had  been giving Invitational conferences for  each of four years and in 72 they  declared that was the last time they  were going to do that ladies and  gentlemen you take it from here and at  that last Duke conference in 72 there  was formed an association  the educational institutions that now  were in the process of turning out  physician's assistants and while he was  in the bathroom Fred Sadler was elected  president of the association of  physician assistant programs now we have  a professional association association  of educational programs under the AMA  auspice an accreditation system of those  programs and the final of link the final  leg of that chair is an accreditation or  a certification system in the public  interest in 1974 even though the first  examination has already been given by  the national board of medical examiners  with participation of specialty  societies state medical boards military  veterans other interested parties 14  national organizations came together to  create the national commission on  certification of physicians assistants  for which I was elected the president so  here we are today 40 years after the  most significant year in the history of  the physician assistant professional  development at both Yale and nationwide  I'll now turn this over to others who  will give you more details about how  this developed particularly here at Yale  and I thank you it's been a pleasure
 

